Standard Exercism circular buffer. Implement a templated class in the header. Note that -Werror -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic is used. Use std::vector<T> storage, with head/tail/count.

Note: The test uses `buffer.write("hello")` with a const char* — the buffer is circular_buffer<std::string>, and write takes T by value or const reference. When passing a const char* literal to write("hello"), T is std::string, so it gets converted. No problem.

Implementation:

```cpp
template <typename T>
class circular_buffer {
public:
    explicit circular_buffer(size_t capacity)
        : storage_(capacity), head_(0), tail_(0), count_(0) {}

    void write(const T& item) {
        if (count_ == storage_.size())
            throw std::domain_error("Buffer is full");
        storage_[head_] = item;
        head_ = (head_ + 1) % storage_.size();
        ++count_;
    }

    void overwrite(const T& item) {
        if (count_ == storage_.size()) {
            storage_[tail_] = item;
            tail_ = (tail_ + 1) % storage_.size();
        } else {
            write(item);
        }
    }

    T read() {
        if (count_ == 0)
            throw std::domain_error("Buffer is empty");
        T item = std::move(storage_[tail_]);
        tail_ = (tail_ + 1) % storage_.size();
        --count_;
        return item;
    }

    void clear() {
        head_ = tail_ = 0;
        count_ = 0;
        storage_.clear(); // hmm, capacity is lost
    }
```

Note: clear() must not lose capacity. Just reset head/tail/count. But the test "initial_clear_does_not_affect_wrapping_around" — even after clear, writes should work. Resetting indices is sufficient.

Edge case: capacity 0? Not tested. But modulo by zero would crash. The tests only use capacity >= 1. Could guard against it, but keep it simple. Actually, to be safe, could handle capacity 0 gracefully, but the tests don't require it. Keep it simple.

-Werror with -Wpedantic: watch out for unused parameters and such. Should be fine.

The cpp file just includes the header.

